  <p>For mRNA vaccines [<span data-field="bioNTech_pfizer_note">{{ bioNTech_pfizer_note }}</span> and <span data-field="moderna_note">{{ moderna_note }}</span>], conservative estimates for the effectiveness are <span data-field="effectiveness_percentage_1">{{ effectiveness_percentage_1 }}</span>% after one dose and <span data-field="effectiveness_percentage_2">{{ effectiveness_percentage_2 }}</span>% after two doses. The second estimate integrates the effect of waning</p>
</div>
<div class="page">
  <p>immunity, as the protection against severe outcomes is higher than <span data-field="immunity_threshold_1">{{ immunity_threshold_1 }}</span>% up to <span data-field="immunity_threshold_2">{{ immunity_threshold_2 }}</span> weeks after inoculation and above <span data-field="immunity_threshold_3">{{ immunity_threshold_3 }}</span>% for AstraZeneca's vaccine, comparable estimates are <span data-field="comparable_effectiveness_1">{{ comparable_effectiveness_1 }}</span>% after one dose and <span data-field="comparable_effectiveness_2">{{ comparable_effectiveness_2 }}</span>% after two doses. Here, waning immunity explains the lower effectiveness of two AstraZeneca doses versus one. Finally, for Janssen Pharmaceutica NV, the effectiveness after the single dose is estimated at <span data-field="janssen_effectiveness">{{ janssen_effectiveness }}</span>%.</p>
  <p>The overall vaccine effectiveness against hospital admissions, ICU admissions, and deaths is approximately the same and is also similar across France, Germany, and Italy, namely <span data-field="overall_effectiveness_percentage">{{ overall_effectiveness_percentage }}</span>% protection after one dose and <span data-field="overall_effectiveness_percentage_2">{{ overall_effectiveness_percentage_2 }}</span>% after two doses. We do not include the additional protection provided by boosters, as the calculations we perform are only concerned with individuals who were not fully vaccinated before the COVID certificate; therefore, they were not eligible for a booster shot over the period of study.</p>
  <p><strong>Realised health outcomes by vaccine status.</strong> Let <span data-field="X_t">{{ X_t }}</span> denote the realised health outcome [ie hospital admissions and patients, ICU admissions and patients, and deaths, for a given country] at time <span data-field="t">{{ t }}</span>, and let <span data-field="X_t(v)">{{ X_t(v) }}</span> denote the same outcome by vaccine status <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> ∈ {0, 1, 2}. When the data by vaccine status are not available, we can derive them from Bayes' rule and the level of protection against the health outcome by vaccine status, <span data-field="beta_v">{{ beta_v }}</span>(v). The <span data-field="X_t(v)">{{ X_t(v) }}</span> satisfies the following linear system:</p>
  <p><span data-field="X_t(v)">{{ X_t(v) }}</span> = <span data-field="beta_v">{{ beta_v }}</span> * <span data-field="X_t(0)">{{ X_t(0) }}</span> + <span data-field="beta_v_prime">{{ beta_v_prime }}</span> * <span data-field="X_t(1)">{{ X_t(1) }}</span> + <span data-field="beta_v_double_prime">{{ beta_v_double_prime }}</span> * <span data-field="X_t(2)">{{ X_t(2) }}</span>, for all <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> ∈ {0, 1, 2}.</p>
  <p>This system admits a unique solution, given by</p>
  <p><span data-field="X_t(v)">{{ X_t(v) }}</span> = <span data-field="X_t_hat">{{ X_t_hat }}</span> * <span data-field="beta_v_hat">{{ beta_v_hat }}</span>, for all <span data-field="v">{{ v }}</span> ∈ {0, 1, 2}.</p>
  <p>Note that vaccine uptake has been lagged by <span data-field="lag_days">{{ lag_days }}</span> days to account for the lag between infection and the health outcome, <span data-field="I_lag">{{ I_lag }}</span>, and <span data-field="I_lag_prime">{{ I_lag_prime }}</span>, the lag between vaccination and full effectiveness, <span data-field="I_vaccine">{{ I_vaccine }}</span>, and the duration of the health hazard, <span data-field="I_stay">{{ I_stay }}</span> and <span data-field="I_stay_prime">{{ I_stay_prime }}</span>, which are only relevant for hospital admissions and ICU patients. For example, for a patient who is in an ICU at time <span data-field="t">{{ t }}</span> on average, their admission occurred at time <span data-field="t_minus_lag_icu">{{ t_minus_lag_icu }}</span>, their infection at time <span data-field="t_minus_lag_infection">{{ t_minus_lag_infection }}</span>, and at that time <span data-field="t_minus_lag_icu_infection">{{ t_minus_lag_icu_infection }}</span>, people were fully protected by vaccination.</p>
</div>
<div class="page">
  <h3>D. Impact on the economy</h3>
  <p>We estimate the average impact of a marginal increase in vaccination rates on economic activity using two-way fixed-effect regressions. The measure of economic activity used in this paper is the OECD Weekly Tracker, a proxy of weekly GDP relative to the pre-crisis trend, which is available for 46 countries with no population delay (Methods A). It is regressed on vaccination rates along with controls as well as country and week fixed effects. To estimate the average total effect of vaccination on GDP, we use the following closed-form model:</p>
  <p><span data-field="T_i,w">{{ T_i,w }}</span> = <span data-field="beta_1">{{ beta_1 }}</span> * <span data-field="I_i,w">{{ I_i,w }}</span> + <span data-field="beta_2">{{ beta_2 }}</span> * <span data-field="I_i,w_lag">{{ I_i,w_lag }}</span> + <span data-field="I_i,w_lag_prime">{{ I_i,w_lag_prime }}</span> * <span data-field="I_i,w_lag_prime_prime">{{ I_i,w_lag_prime_prime }}</span> + <span data-field="sigma_w">{{ sigma_w }}</span>.</p>
  <p>Weekly GDP is proxied by the Tracker <span data-field="Tracker_name">{{ Tracker_name }}</span> and is regressed on the share of vaccinated people lagged by <span data-field="l_weeks">{{ l_weeks }}</span> [i.e., <span data-field="l_weeks_value">{{ l_weeks_value }}</span>], as well as three vectors of controls, week, and country dummies. The first controls vector <span data-field="controls_vector_1">{{ controls_vector_1 }}</span> includes lagged cases, deaths, reproduction rate, and mobility index, which may have impacted both past vaccine uptake decisions and present weekly GDP. The model also reverts confounding effects that could emerge from trade and other spillover due to the relative synchronicity of vaccination campaigns across countries by controlling for vaccination and deaths in the main trading partners [<span data-field="trading_partners_note">{{ trading_partners_note }}</span>]. The vector <span data-field="X_i,w">{{ X_i,w }}</span> is the weighted average of vaccination rates and deaths in country <span data-field="i"">{{ i }}</span>'s main 10 trading partners, i.e.</p>
  <p><span data-field="V_i,w">{{ V_i,w }}</span> = <span data-field="sum_over_j">{{ sum_over_j }}</span> <span data-field="V_j,w">{{ V_j,w }}</span> * <span data-field="w_j">{{ w_j }}</span>,</p>
  <p>where <span data-field="V_j,w">{{ V_j,w }}</span> is the vaccination rate in trading partner <span data-field="j"">{{ j }}</span> and <span data-field="w_j">{{ w_j }}</span> is the share of exports from country <span data-field="i"">{{ i }}</span> to trade partner <span data-field="j"">{{ j }}</span> in total exports from country <span data-field="i"">{{ i }}</span>. The same formula is used to build the vector of weighted average death rates in trading partners. Last, the model includes the vector of average weekly temperatures <span data-field="Z_i,w">{{ Z_i,w }}</span>, which can influence virus transmission.</p>
</div>
<div class="page">
  <p>The model is estimated using data from <span data-field="data_period">{{ data_period }}</span> OECD and <span data-field="data_period_2">{{ data_period_2 }}</span> countries (see Table 4). Denote by <span data-field="p">{{ p }}</span> <span data-field="p_value_3">{{ p_value_3 }}</span> <span data-field="p_value_4">{{ p_value_4 }}</span> and <span data-field="p_value_5">{{ p_value_5 }}</span>. The average effect of a 1 p.p. increase in the share of vaccinated people after a month is <span data-field="avg_effect_1">{{ avg_effect_1 }}</span> p.p. in weekly GDP. This order of magnitude seems plausible and implies that the impact was permanent, that 100% vaccination uptake would increase GDP by <span data-field="max_gdp_increase">{{ max_gdp_increase }}</span> p.p. which broadly corresponds to 85% of the average GDP loss suffered in 2020 by the countries in the sample. This is consistent with the notion that a complete vaccination would not be sufficient to return to pre-crisis trends due to partial vaccine effectiveness and the waning of vaccine-provided immunity. Adding controls for deaths and vaccination in trade partners decreases the main estimate from <span data-field="main_estimate_1">{{ main_estimate_1 }}</span>% to <span data-field="main_estimate_2">{{ main_estimate_2 }}</span>% by partially accounting for the confounding effect of trade spillover, finally, the third column models the direct effect by controlling for current cases, deaths, and reproduction rates. This indicates that 83% of the total economic effect of vaccination is through the direct effect on individual behaviour, while the remaining 17% is related to the effect through the impact on virus circulation, however, we do not estimate the indirect effects independently, as we do not estimate a policy response function.</p>
